From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] HFR VRR: the 3D mode is Active 3D for VRR displays

The 3D mode took its name from the 1080p 120 Hz base it first ran on.
It now runs on the sink's fastest 1080p (144, 165, 240 Hz), so the name
drops the rate; the shutter add-on takes the same one.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -22734,7 +22734,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Stereoscopic 3D mode: one eye per refresh at a high refresh rate with VRR, for shutter glasses on a 2D display
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#60030"
-msgid "Active 3D for 120 Hz VRR displays"
+msgid "Active 3D for VRR displays"
 msgstr ""
 
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
@@ -22752,7 +22752,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Description of setting with label #60031 "3D glasses delay"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#60033"
-msgid "Only in Active 3D for 120 Hz VRR displays: moves the shutter glasses' eye switch after each refresh, for an emitter driven from the box. Tune it during a 3D film: through one lens the other eye's picture should be as faint as it gets, and as faint at the top of the screen as at the bottom. A screen that takes most of the frame to draw leaves some in lenses that stay open the whole frame, and this only moves it; glasses or an emitter that can shorten the opening can remove it, with this delay placing the opening once the screen has settled. A whole frame either way covers every timing, swapped eyes included: past half a frame the glasses take the other eye, so turning on Invert stereoscopic 3D mode is never needed for them."
+msgid "Only in Active 3D for VRR displays: moves the shutter glasses' eye switch after each refresh, for an emitter driven from the box. Tune it during a 3D film: through one lens the other eye's picture should be as faint as it gets, and as faint at the top of the screen as at the bottom. A screen that takes most of the frame to draw leaves some in lenses that stay open the whole frame, and this only moves it; glasses or an emitter that can shorten the opening can remove it, with this delay placing the opening once the screen has settled. A whole frame either way covers every timing, swapped eyes included: past half a frame the glasses take the other eye, so turning on Invert stereoscopic 3D mode is never needed for them."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Heading of the slider for the playing film's 3D depth
